{
  "_doc": "N-259. Replayed by ../checkmultisig-onchain.test.ts through every SDK ANF interpreter in on-chain mode (TS in-process, the six drivers via --mode=on-chain) and through @bsv/sdk Spend.validate() on the compiled probe ../programs/CheckMultiSigOrProbe.runar.ts, i.e. assert(checkMultiSig([s1, s2], [pk1, pk2, pk3]) || checkSig(sigB, pubB)). `sighash` is the digest Spend computes for that probe's locking script in ScriptExecutionContract's fixed spend context (version 1, SIGHASH_ALL|FORKID, no other inputs or outputs); the test recomputes it on every run, so every signature here verifies under Spend too. Keys: k1/k2/k3 = priv 0x11../0x22../0x33.., pubB = priv 0x44.., the out-of-set key = 0x55..; signatures are RFC 6979 low-S ECDSA plus a 0x41 sighash-type byte (the high-S case is the n-s twin of k3's). A malformed key is the compressed key with its prefix replaced by 0x05. `outcome` is every SDK interpreter's verdict: succeeds (sigB is empty, so only the multisig can satisfy the assert), false (the multisig pushes false and sigB carries the spend), assert (nothing verifies), aborts (with `reason`). OP_CHECKMULTISIG examines s2 against pk3 first, then walks both lists toward s1/pk1, applies the encoding gates only to the pairs it examines, and stops once fewer keys remain than signatures. A `nullfail` case aborts on a BSV node under NULLFAIL (BIP146: a failed OP_CHECKMULTISIG with any non-empty signature); Spend has no NULLFAIL and pushes false, so for those cases Spend is NOT the oracle.",
